if you delete an EC2 from the Terraform state file but leave the EC2 resource in your .tf code, Terraform will think the EC2 is not being managed.

Example:

AWS:
EC2 i-12345 → exists ✅

State:
EC2 i-12345 → removed ❌

main.tf:
resource "aws_instance" "app" { ... } → still exists

Now run:

terraform plan

Terraform sees:

Configuration says: "I need an EC2"
State says:         "I don't have this EC2"

So Terraform will normally plan:

+ create aws_instance.app

Then terraform apply can create another EC2.

So you could end up with:

AWS
├── Old EC2 i-12345  ← still there
└── New EC2 i-67890  ← Terraform creates
If you want Terraform to manage the existing EC2

Instead of deleting it from state, you should import the existing EC2 into Terraform state:

terraform import aws_instance.app i-12345

Then Terraform knows:

main.tf
   ↕
state
   ↕
existing EC2 i-12345

Key point: terraform state rm means "forget this resource", not "delete the AWS resource."
