Let's put User Data, local-exec, and remote-exec together because they are easy to confuse.

1. User Data

User Data runs inside the EC2 when the EC2 starts.

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  user_data = <<-EOF
    #!/bin/bash

    apt update -y
    apt install nginx -y
    systemctl start nginx
    systemctl enable nginx
  EOF
}

Flow:
Terraform machine
       |
       | creates EC2
       ↓
     AWS EC2
       |
       | EC2 starts
       ↓
   User Data runs
       |
       ↓
 install nginx
 start nginx

So User Data is executed on the EC2, not on your laptop.


2. local-exec
local-exec runs a command on the machine where Terraform is running.
Example:
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo EC2 created"
  }
}

If you run Terraform on your laptop:

Your laptop
   ↓
Terraform
   ↓
Creates EC2
   ↓
local-exec
   ↓
Runs on YOUR LAPTOP

For example:

provisioner "local-exec" {
  command = "echo Hello"
}

The echo command runs on your computer.

If Terraform is running in a CI/CD server:

CI/CD server
     ↓
Terraform
     ↓
local-exec
     ↓
Runs on CI/CD server

So local means local to the Terraform execution machine.




3. remote-exec
remote-exec runs commands inside the remote machine, such as your EC2.
Example:
resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "sudo apt update -y",
      "sudo apt install nginx -y",
      "sudo systemctl start nginx"
    ]
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "ubuntu"
    private_key = file("my-key.pem")
    host        = self.public_ip
  }
}

Flow:

Your laptop
    ↓
Terraform
    ↓
Creates EC2
    ↓
SSH connection
    ↓
EC2
    ↓
remote-exec commands
    ↓
Install nginx


Main difference
                 WHERE DOES THE COMMAND RUN?
                 
User Data       → Inside EC2
local-exec      → Terraform machine
remote-exec     → Remote EC2

Easy example

Suppose you want to install nginx.

User Data:

user_data = <<-EOF
  #!/bin/bash
  apt update -y
  apt install nginx -y
EOF

Runs:

EC2 → nginx installed

Remote-exec:

provisioner "remote-exec" {
  inline = [
    "sudo apt update -y",
    "sudo apt install nginx -y"
  ]
}

Runs:

EC2 → nginx installed

So both can configure the EC2, but they work differently.





Then why use User Data instead of remote-exec?

For initial EC2 setup, User Data is usually preferred.

Terraform
   ↓
Create EC2
   ↓
User Data
   ↓
Initial configuration

You don't need Terraform to connect to the EC2 using SSH.

This is especially useful when you don't want to open SSH port 22.

remote-exec generally needs connectivity and credentials, such as SSH.

One complete example
resource "aws_instance" "app" {

  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  # Runs inside EC2
  user_data = <<-EOF
    #!/bin/bash
    apt update -y
    apt install nginx -y
    systemctl enable nginx
    systemctl start nginx
  EOF

  # Runs on the Terraform machine
  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "echo EC2 has been created"
  }
}

And if you use remote-exec:

resource "aws_instance" "app" {

  ami           = "ami-xxxxxxxx"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  provisioner "remote-exec" {

    inline = [
      "sudo apt update -y",
      "sudo apt install nginx -y",
      "sudo systemctl start nginx"
    ]
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "ubuntu"
    private_key = file("my-key.pem")
    host        = self.public_ip
  }
}
Remember this for interviews
Method	Runs where?	Common use
User Data	EC2	Initial EC2 setup
local-exec	Terraform machine	Run a local command/script
remote-exec	Remote EC2	Run commands on remote machine

Important: local-exec and remote-exec are Terraform provisioners. User Data is an EC2 startup mechanism provided through the AWS instance configuration.



if User Data can do the initial setup, why do we need remote-exec?

The answer is: remote-exec is useful when you need Terraform to connect to an already-created machine and run commands on it.

So when would you use remote-exec?

For example, suppose the EC2 already exists and you want Terraform to execute a specific command on it as part of a Terraform operation.

Existing EC2
     ↓
Terraform connects
     ↓
Run command
     ↓
Change something
However, in modern Terraform practice, remote-exec is usually a last resort. Prefer:

User Data/cloud-init → initial setup
Ansible/configuration management → ongoing server configuration
AWS Systems Manager → remote administration without SSH
Terraform → creating/managing infrastructure

